```python
transitions(
    data,
    variable,
    *,
    k=5,
    scheme='quantiles',
    bins=None,
    per_year=True,
    relative=False,
    title=None,
    ref=None,
)
```

Markov transitions between classes of a variable's distribution: how mobile are the units?

Every unit is assigned to one of ``k`` classes in each year, and all the moves from
one year to the next are counted in a transition matrix: the probability of being
in each class next year, given this year's class. Needs the ``[dynamics]`` extra.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name     | Type        | Description                                                                                                                                                             | Default       |
|----------|-------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
| data     |             | A dataset id, or a panel with ``geo_id`` and ``year``, e.g. from ``load``                                                                                               | _required_    |
| variable | str         | The numeric column to follow, observed in at least two years; the units without a value in every year are left out                                                      | _required_    |
| k        | int         | Number of classes                                                                                                                                                       | `5`           |
| scheme   | str         | How classes are formed: ``"quantiles"`` (equal numbers of units), ``"equal_interval"`` or ``"fisher_jenks"``                                                            | `'quantiles'` |
| bins     |             | Upper bounds of fixed classes, the same in every year (replaces ``k``, ``scheme`` and ``per_year``)                                                                     | `None`        |
| per_year | bool        | Classify each year on its own, so a class is a position within that year's distribution (default); ``False`` classifies all years together, so classes are fixed levels | `True`        |
| relative | bool        | Divide the values by each year's mean first                                                                                                                             | `False`       |
| title    | str \| None | The figure title                                                                                                                                                        | `None`        |

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type          | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |
|--------|---------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | result object | A result with ``.p`` (the transition probabilities, rows = this year's class) and ``.counts``, ``.fig`` (the matrix as a heatmap), ``.gt`` (mobility indices, long-run shares and expected stays), ``.df`` (one row per unit and year with its ``state``), ``.states``, ``.steady_state`` (the long-run distribution), ``.sojourn`` (expected years in each class), the mobility indices ``.shorrocks``, ``.prais`` and ``.bartholomew``, ``.n_transitions`` and ``.interpret()``. |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q "geo4bolivia[dynamics]"
import geo4bolivia as geo

res = geo.transitions("ntl", "ln_NTLpc", k=5)
print(res.interpret())
res.fig
```

